    <!-- Techniques -->
    <string name="technique_pixelate">Pixelate</string>
    <string name="technique_pixelate_desc">Turns faces into big colour blocks. Strong (few big blocks) is very reliable. Light can still be recognised by strong AI, especially after the photo is resized.</string>
    <string name="technique_blur">Blur</string>
    <string name="technique_blur_desc">Smears facial detail. Fools weaker AI, but in our tests a strong recognition model still matched about 3 in 4 heavily blurred faces. For real protection use Pixelate on Strong or Solid mask.</string>
    <string name="technique_mask">Solid mask</string>
    <string name="technique_mask_desc">Covers faces completely. The most reliable option.</string>
    <string name="technique_dead_pixels">Dead pixels &amp; noise</string>
    <string name="technique_dead_pixels_desc">Stuck pixels, colour noise and scanlines. Looks like a glitch; on its own it\'s the weakest protection, so use Strong.</string>
    <string name="technique_landmarks">Feature bars</string>
    <string name="technique_landmarks_desc">Bars across the eyes, nose and mouth, the parts face detectors rely on most. Leaves the rest of the face visible.</string>

    <string name="technique_invisible">Invisible cloak</string>
    <string name="technique_invisible_desc">A faint trained pattern and a tiny change in face shape that confuse recognition AI while the photo looks almost the same. Not a guarantee: newer AI can learn to see through it.</string>

